\begin{align*}
r & = \sqrt{(1)^2 + (-1)^2} \\
& = \sqrt{2}.
\end{align*}
Next, we find $\theta$ using the relationship $\tan{\theta} = \frac{y}{x}$, which for our point yields
\begin{align*}
\tan{\theta} &= \frac{-1}{1} \\ 
\Rightarrow\qquad \theta &= \tan^{-1}(-1).
\end{align*}
Since $\tan^{-1}(-1) = -\frac{\pi}{4}$, but we need $0 \le \theta < 2 \pi$, we find that $\theta = 2\pi - \frac{\pi}{4} = \frac{7\pi}{4}$. Finally, the $z$-coordinate remains unchanged, so $z = -6$. Therefore, the cylindrical coordinates are $\boxed{\left(\sqrt{2}, \frac{7\pi}{4}, -6\right)}$. </think> <answer> $\left(\sqrt{2}, \frac{7\pi}{4}, -6\right)$ </answer>

