response:
 First, let's call the earliest arrival Xena.  Now draw Xena's time on the x-axis, and Annie's time on the y-axis.  Each possible pair of arrival times is going to be uniquely described by a single point in the coordinate plane, because each axis is only 2 hours long.
What we want to know is the probability that the regions covered by Xena and Annie's arrival times overlap.  This is the the same as the probability that there's some point that both Xena and Annie are staying at.  We can draw this condition graphically.  Annie sees Xena at the party if there's some interval of time of length 45 minutes [e.g., 2:10-2:55], that overlap her time interval [e.g., 2:00-4:45].  If you've ever been late to the "faux-police" meeting, you've probably felt this sort of thing.
The condition that they both stay at the party is that their intervals overlap by at least 45 minutes.  Graphically, that means the two points in the square $(0,0)$ to $(120,120)$ need to be within a diamond of height 45 and width 315, centered at $(60,60)$.
So, what's the area of this diamond?  Well, it's 2 in AoPSWiki style:

[asy]
size(3inch);
draw((0,0)--(120,0),black);
draw((0,0)--(0,120),black);
draw((0,120)--(120,120),black);
draw((120,120)--(120,0),black);
draw((0,0)--(120,120),black);
fill((20,20)--(60,120)--(100,20)--cycle,black);
label("45",(60,20),SE);
label("45",(60,100),NW);
draw((60,20)--(60,120),dash);
draw((60,120)--(100,120),dash);
draw((100,20)--(100,120),dash);
draw((60,60)--(100,60),lineticksmargin=2pt,Arrow);
[/asy]

Referring to the picture, the side with the thick line for Annie has length $120-2\times 45=30$, so half that is $15$, so the area is $15\times 315=4725$.  There are $120\times 120=14400$ possible arrival times, so the probability is $4725/14400=7/16$.
</think> <answer>  $\boxed{\frac{7}{16}}$ </answer>

